# 0119 makes the composed capability limits reach WebGL on ANGLE/Metal, which
# is the backend the macOS artifact runs. It replaces 0103's backend test --
# clamp the claim where the backend honours what it reports, serve it whole
# where it does not -- with a test on the kind of GL context asking: a
# WebGL-compatibility context is served the claim on every backend, and the
# compositor, raster and Skia Ganesh keep the host's own caps, because all
# three come through the same six query entry points and only the first is a
# fingerprint.
#
# After 0112, which it rewrites rather than extends. Every line it deletes is
# 0112's: RendererIsNonEnforcingRasteriser, BackendEnforcesGlLimits and the
# renderer read behind RealGLApi::ApostateEnforces. It keeps 0112's table, its
# floor direction, its fractional map and all six of its hooks untouched, and
# it changes the sense and the name of the one boolean they pass around. Its
# compose.cc hunk rewrites the --fingerprint-explain sentence 0112 put there,
# so it cannot sit before it.
#
# After 0117, for one reason and not a semantic one: 0117 inserts into
# base/apostate/compose.cc above and below the GL-limits block this patch
# edits, so the context of the compose.cc hunk is 0117's file. Nothing else
# connects them -- 0117 is a font-render-params projection and reads no GL
# key.
#
# ANGLE's ApplyProfilePointSizeCaps and ApplyProfileIntegerCaps, added by
# 0039, are deliberately not touched, so nothing orders it against that patch
# either: those min-clamps are what keeps the backend's own enforcement real
# and the residual honest. Independent of 0116 and 0118, which are a data-table
# axis and the WebRTC egress families.
0119-gl-serve-the-anchor-limits-to-webgl-on-every-backend.patch
